ry responses and alterations in hemodynamics contribute to myocardial stunning and dysfunction.\",\n                \"Chronic inflammatory states and oncology patients are at higher risk for TTC because:\",\n                \"- Chronic inflammation leads to endothelial dysfunction, making the heart more susceptible to stress-induced injury.\",\n                \"- Cancer-related interventions such as chemotherapy and stress from disease progression amplify adrenergic responses, contributing to TTC onset.\"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    \"explanation\": {\n        \"diagnostic_value_topic_1\": \"Differentiating TTC from ischemic MI is critical in this case. The transient resolution of left ventricular dysfunction and the characteristic apical ballooning exceeding a single coronary artery distribution strongly support TTC over MI.\",\n        \"diagnostic_value_topic_2\": \"The patient’s recent major surgical history and chronic inflammatory state due to cancer-related treatments are significant risk factors for stress-induced cardiomyopathy (TTC). These align with the pathophysiology of catecholamine surge and myocardial dysfunction described above, reinforcing the TTC diagnosis.\"\n    },\n    \"selected_option\": \"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.95,\n    \"self_questions\": [\n        \"Does the echocardiographic evidence definitively rule out coronary artery-associated ischemia?\",\n        \"Can the stressors identified in the patient's case fully account for the Takotsubo cardiomyopathy diagnosis?\"\n    ],\n    \"needs_retrieval\": false\n}\n```",
