ossible. This imaging technique is more conclusive when paired with pathologic assessment from biopsy or autopsy.\",\n                \"In systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)-related vasculitis, VW-MRI often shows concentric or eccentric wall thickening with enhancement in medium-to-small vessels.\"\n            ],\n            \"relevance\": \"The lack of vessel wall enhancement in this case strongly suggests the absence of active small vessel vasculitis, aligning with the CAPS diagnosis.\"\n        },\n        \"topic_3\": {\n            \"title\": \"Clinical and laboratory indicators differentiating CAPS from other SLE-related complications\",\n            \"content\": [\n                \"Catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome (CAPS) is characterized by rapidly progressive multiorgan failure due to diffuse microthrombosis. It is often triggered by conditions such as infection, malignancy, or an SLE flare.\",\n                \"Indicators of CAPS include clinical evidence of acute multiorgan thrombotic events (e.g., brain, kidney, and lungs), positive antiphospholipid antibodies (e.g., anticardiolipin antibody), and thrombotic microangiopathy on histology.\",\n                \"SLE flares presenting with encephalitis or vasculitis typically show elevated inflammatory markers (e.g., ESR or CRP), leukocytosis in the CSF, or abnormal vessel wall imaging. These were not observed in this case.\",\n                \"Libman-Sacks endocarditis, found at autopsy, is a common comorbidity of CAPS and SLE.\"\n            ],\n            \"relevance\": \"This helps confirm CAPS as the primary etiology as systemic microthrombosis, organ ischemia, and positive anticardiolipin antibodies are all consistent with this condition. Lack of inflammatory markers, vasculitis-specific CSF changes, or vessel wall enhancement further supports the exclusion of competing diagnoses.\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"interpretation\": {\n        \"how_it_helps\": [\n            \"The imaging differentiating patterns clarified that the diffuse microvascular ischemia observed on MRI is characteristic of CAPS-related microthrombi, not septic embolism.\",\n            \"The absence of vessel wall enhancement on VW-MRI effectively ruled out active vasculitis as a primary cause of the presentation.\",\n            \"CAPS-specific systemic manifestations, supported by clinical signs (e.g., multiorgan failure, neurological symptoms) and pathological findings (e.g., Libman-Sacks endocarditis, widespread microthrombosis), confirm the diagnosis while distinguishing it from other SLE complications.\"\n        ],\n        \"impact\": \"These retrieved findings validate the initial hypothesis and strengthen diagnostic confidence in identifying multiorgan ischemia due to CAPS as the diagnosis.\"\n    }\n}\n```",
