ment is rare and unlikely.\",\n            \"Tuberculoid leprosy typically presents with hypopigmented anesthetic plaques and peripheral nerve damage, including sensory loss. The involvement of the palms and soles is uncommon, and alopecia is not typically patchy as seen in syphilis.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    \"self-questions\": [\n        \"How does secondary syphilis rash differ from other similar conditions like tinea corporis or leprosy?\",\n        \"What laboratory tests are most reliable in confirming secondary syphilis, especially in a patient with HIV?\",\n        \"Are there any other systemic features of secondary syphilis that support this diagnosis?\"\n    ],\n    \"diagnostic_importance\": [\n        \"The circinate lesions on the palms and soles, combined with patchy alopecia and systemic symptoms, strongly align with secondary syphilis as described in retrieved knowledge.\",\n        \"The direct visualization of Treponema pallidum through microscopy or serologic tests will confirm the diagnosis, eliminating doubts related to tinea corporis or leprosy differentiation.\"\n    ],\n    \"needs_final_synthesis\": true\n}\n```",
