ion (PAS-positive material).\",\n                \"- GM-CSF autoantibodies are diagnostic for autoimmune PAP.\",\n                \"2. Pneumocystis Pneumonia (PCP):\",\n                \"- Occurs in immunocompromised patients (e.g., HIV/AIDS).\",\n                \"- Crazy-paving due to alveolar and interstitial edema with organisms identifiable on stains.\",\n                \"3. Organizing Pneumonia (OP):\",\n                \"- Migratory consolidations dominate rather than diffuse crazy-paving.\",\n                \"- Associated with organizing fibrotic plugs in respiratory bronchioles and alveoli.\",\n                \"4. Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis (HP):\",\n                \"- Often shows centrilobular nodules and mosaic attenuation patterns rather than diffuse crazy-paving.\",\n                \"- History of antigen exposure may be present.\",\n                \"5. Lipoid Pneumonia:\",\n                \"- Crazy-paving accompanied by fat-density areas due to aspirated oils identified on imaging.\"\n            ],\n            \"source\": \"Current medical imaging guidelines and diagnostic textbooks.\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"diagnosis_support\": [\n        \"The diagnostic criteria for autoimmune PAP match this case: the patient presents with hallmark HRCT crazy-paving patterns, elevated GM-CSF autoantibodies, and PAS-positive surfactant accumulation in biopsies.\",\n        \"Distinguishing features for crazy-paving patterns rule out alternative diagnoses such as PCP, OP, or HP. Relevant findings such as surfactant accumulation and the absence of infectious or antigen exposure history align exclusively with PAP.\"\n    ],\n    \"self_questions_addressed\": [\n        \"How specific are the imaging patterns (crazy-paving) for PAP among other differential diagnoses?\",\n        \"What role do GM-CSF autoantibody levels play in confirming autoimmune PAP?\"\n    ],\n    \"needs_clarification\": false\n}\n```",
