ity of vision loss rather than progression, indicating that damage occurs acutely upon exposure to poppers.\"\n        ],\n        \"Other substances or factors leading to similar IS/OS junction damage\": [\n            \"Substances such as tamoxifen, hydroxychloroquine, and phosphodiesterase inhibitors (e.g., sildenafil) are known to cause toxic maculopathies, resulting in IS/OS damage with varying accompanying fundoscopic and imaging findings.\",\n            \"Solar retinopathy also causes photoreceptor damage, showing similar IS/OS disruption; however, this is typically preceded by a history of intense light exposure.\",\n            \"Hereditary conditions like pattern dystrophies or cone-rod dystrophies involve IS/OS damage, but these are usually associated with a family history of vision problems or detectable genetic mutations.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    \"relevance_to_diagnosis\": [\n        \"Poppers maculopathy is differentiated from other toxic maculopathies and hereditary retinal dystrophies by the presence of localized IS/OS disruption, yellow foveal spots, and a history of poppers exposure.\",\n        \"The prognosis data support the observation that the patient's visual acuity did not improve despite cessation, strengthening the likelihood of poppers maculopathy.\",\n        \"The absence of a family history of hereditary disease and lack of features such as bull's-eye maculopathy or fluid accumulation in the retina help rule out other potential causes like Stargardt disease, CSCR, or chloroquine-induced maculopathy.\"\n    ],\n    \"confidence_change\": \"Increased\",\n    \"self_questions\": [\n        \"Could additional imaging modalities, such as fundus autofluorescence, confirm the absence of retinal dystrophic features or toxic maculopathy?\",\n        \"What ancillary tests (e.g., electrophysiology, genetics) may definitively exclude hereditary conditions as a differential?\"\n    ],\n    \"needs_retrieval\": true\n}\n```",
