r infiltrative diseases, or when the CMR findings are inconclusive.\",\n            \"In clinical practice, CMR is preferred for non-invasive diagnostic confirmation, particularly in suspected myocarditis cases with mild forms or when ventricular function is preserved. However, when CMR findings are equivocal, biopsy may be required.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    \"application_to_diagnosis\": {\n        \"diagnostic_workflows_application\": [\n            \"The patient's elevated troponin coupled with diffuse ECG abnormalities raised concerns about potential myocardial injury. Given his high GRACE score, clinicians appropriately commenced antiplatelet and anticoagulation therapy and performed coronary angiography to rule out ACS.\",\n            \"Following normal angiography findings, coronary ischemia was excluded, leading to the consideration of myocarditis. Subsequently, CMR imaging revealed subepicardial LGE of the apex and inferolateral wall, which strongly supported the myocarditis diagnosis.\",\n            \"Given mild left ventricular hypertrophy and normal ejection fraction, the diagnosis of SARS-CoV-2-associated myocarditis was confirmed based on imaging findings and the absence of other identifiable causes of myocarditis.\"\n        ],\n        \"cmr_vs_biopsy_application\": [\n            \"In this case, CMR played a pivotal role in non-invasively diagnosing myocarditis. The finding of subepicardial late gadolinium enhancement is characteristic of myocarditis and was sufficient to confirm the diagnosis.\",\n            \"An endomyocardial biopsy was not performed because the patient had no severe symptoms of heart failure or arrhythmias, and CMR findings were conclusive. The decision avoided unnecessary procedural risk while still providing an accurate diagnosis supported by clinical, biochemical, and imaging findings.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    \"conclusion\": \"The retrieved knowledge reaffirms that the protocol followed in this case was appropriate. The 