ity, bones begin to sequester calcium from the circulation, resulting in hypocalcemia.\",\n            \"Low urinary calcium excretion supports the diagnosis of hungry bone syndrome, as calcium is rapidly deposited into the bone rather than being excreted. Biochemical markers typically show suppressed serum calcium and elevated PTH shortly after discontinuation.\"\n        ],\n        \"bone_scintigraphy_changes_in_rhPTH_treatment\": [\n            \"Bone scintigraphy is sensitive to metabolic activity. 'Super bone scan' findings, characterized by diffuse intense uptake in the axial and appendicular skeleton (but with sparing of soft tissues such as the kidneys), are documented in patients undergoing rhPTH therapy for hypoparathyroidism.\",\n            \"Case studies have highlighted that bone scintigraphy findings in rhPTH-treated patients normalize within months to a year after discontinuation of treatment, confirming the transient nature of its effects. Biochemical correlates include reduced turnover markers (e.g., alkaline phosphatase) after cessation.\"\n        ]\n    },\n    \"diagnostic_implications\": {\n        \"pharmacokinetics_of_rhPTH\": \"The short half-life and frequent dosing patterns of rhPTH (1–34) suggest the potential for chronic overstimulation of bone activity if doses accumulate, explaining the imaging findings and elevated markers in this patient.\",\n        \"hungry_bone_syndrome\": \"Upon discontinuation of rhPTH (1–34), the observed transient hypocalcemia and low urinary calcium excretion are characteristics of hungry bone syndrome, confirming hypermetabolic bone activity induced by prolonged therapy.\",\n        \"bone_scintigraphy_findings\": \"The typical 'super bone scan' patterns seen in rhPTH-treated patients provide strong imaging evidence for overstimulation of bone cells due to rhPTH therapy. The normalization of scans after discontinuing treatment supports this conclusion.\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": 0.95,\n    \"self_quest