ferentiating Sporotrichoid Patterns in Bacterial, Fungal, and Non-Infectious Etiologies\",\n            \"content\": [\n                \"Sporotrichoid lesions, characterized by nodules ascending along lymphatic channels, are most commonly caused by infections.\",\n                \"Infections causing this pattern include Mycobacterium marinum, Sporothrix schenckii (fungal), Nocardia species (bacterial), and occasionally non-infectious conditions like eosinophilic cellulitis.\",\n                \"Key distinguishing factors include the environmental exposure history (aquatic vs soil), microbiological studies (AFB or fungal stains), and culture growth (bacteria vs fungal).\"\n            ]\n        }\n    },\n    \"reasoning\": \"This medical knowledge highlights specific diagnostic features, emphasizes identifying causative organisms, and distinguishes granulomatous changes among bacterial, fungal, and non-infectious causes.\",\n    \"how_it_helps\": [\n        \"The pathophysiology and presentation of Mycobacterium marinum infection emphasize aquatic exposure and sporotrichoid pattern, supporting the initial diagnosis.\",\n        \"Histology findings of granulomas and Ziehl-Neelsen staining help confirm Mycobacterium marinum infection and rule out sarcoidosis or fungal infections.\",\n        \"Differentiation of sporotrichoid patterns clarifies the likely diagnosis based on exposure history and biopsy findings.\"\n    ],\n    \"needs_retrieval\": false\n}\n```",
