ad.\",\n                \"A key difference is complement activity: in pre-eclampsia, complement levels are typically normal or elevated, whereas active lupus nephritis or DAH involves complement activation and depletion, resulting in hypocomplementemia (low C3, C4). This can help distinguish DAH from pulmonary complications of pre-eclampsia.\"\n            ]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"diagnostic_support\": [\n        {\n            \"topic\": \"Diagnostic criteria for diffuse alveolar hemorrhage\",\n            \"supporting_reason\": \"The acute drop in hemoglobin (from 11 g/dL to 8.7 g/dL), chest X-ray findings of diffuse bilateral opacities, hypoxemia, and bronchoscopy confirmation of fresh blood and hemosiderin-laden macrophages align closely with these diagnostic criteria, further strengthening DAH as the diagnosis.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"topic\": \"Overlap of pre-eclampsia with SLE manifestations during pregnancy\",\n            \"supporting_reason\": \"While hypertension, thrombocytopenia, and anemia could be due to HELLP syndrome, the patient's hypocomplementemia (low C3 and C4 levels) strongly points toward immune complex-driven pathogenesis (e.g., lupus nephritis and DAH). This distinguishes DAH from pre-eclampsia with pulmonary edema.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"diagnosis_confirmation\": {\n        \"reasoning\": \"The retrieved knowledge further corroborates that the clinical, laboratory, and imaging findings are consistent with diffuse alveolar hemorrhage secondary to systemic lupus erythematosus and antiphospholipid syndrome. The hypocomplementemia, hemosiderin-containing macrophages, and lack of improvement with diuresis strongly exclude pre-eclampsia or pulmonary edema as primary causes.\",\n        \"final_selection\": \"Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage\",\n        \"confidence\": 0.95\n    }\n}\n```",
